NAME
       gldemo - the software 3D demo

SYNOPSIS
       gldemo [--gears | --cube] [--size WxH] [--full] [--flat]
              [--frames N]

DESCRIPTION
       Draws the classic three meshing gears, or a lit cube, through
       the graphics library in libcervus, which implements a subset of
       the OpenGL 1.1 API. There is no graphics acceleration
       underneath: every triangle is transformed, lit and filled by
       the processor, and the finished picture is copied into the
       framebuffer.

       That library is an independent implementation. It has not been
       through Khronos conformance testing and is not a licensed
       OpenGL implementation. See TRADEMARKS.md.

       A counter in the corner shows the frame rate, and a line at the
       foot shows the scene, the size being rendered and how many
       triangles survived back-face culling in the last frame.

OPTIONS
       --gears     three meshing gears (the default)
       --cube      one rotating cube
       --size WxH  render at this size rather than 800x600
       --full      render at the whole screen size
       --flat      one colour per face instead of shading across it
       --frames N  stop after N frames and print the average rate

KEYS
       g   gears          c   cube
       f   flat or smooth shading
       space  hold the rotation
       q   quit

SPEED
       Only what changed is repainted. The renderer records the left
       and right edge of every row it drew into, and the next frame
       clears and copies just those spans rather than the whole
       picture. That is what keeps the cube cheap - it covers a small
       part of the screen, so almost nothing is touched per frame.

       The cost is close to proportional to the pixels covered, so a
       smaller --size is the lever to pull on a slow machine. --frames
       prints a figure to compare against.

SEE ALSO
       img(1), fbdemo(1)
